\title{Finite-Termination Gradient Descent\\for a Tropical Translation Model}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{August 1, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We study fixed-step training of a scalar tropical translation model under three-sample absolute loss. For ordered reduced targets $a\le m\le c$, the model $f_\theta(z)=z+\theta$ has empirical objective $L(\theta)=|\theta-a|+|\theta-m|+|\theta-c|$, whose unique minimizer is the median $m$. We define clipped tropical descent by moving from an initial value $x$ toward $m$ with cumulative travel budget $n\eta$ after $n$ updates. The resulting iterate satisfies the exact error formula
\[
 |\theta_n-m|=\max\{0,|x-m|-n\eta\}.
\]
For every positive step size, the method terminates at the optimizer after at most $\lceil |x-m|/\eta\rceil$ iterations. Parameter convergence transfers exactly to prediction convergence, since $|f_\theta(z)-f_m(z)|=|\theta-m|$. We derive an explicit excess-risk certificate and show that every clipped update has an exact width-two rectified-linear representation. The results connect tropical optimization, robust median estimation, and ReLU networks.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

The tropical limit replaces smooth nonlinear structure by max-plus, min-plus, and piecewise-linear operations. In learning theory it serves not only as an approximation regime but also as an analytic lens: trajectories that are difficult to describe in a general network may become exact combinations of affine pieces. We develop that perspective for a scalar translation neuron trained on three samples with absolute loss.

The model has a single trainable parameter, but it contains several ingredients that recur in larger piecewise-linear systems: a nonsmooth objective, a robust location statistic, a clipped descent rule, and a representation by rectified linear units. Each can be characterized exactly. Our central identity says that a travel budget $t$ reduces the distance to the median by exactly $t$ until the distance reaches zero. With $t=n\eta$, this yields a complete discrete trajectory and finite termination, rather than only an asymptotic rate.

The translation model is an isometry from parameter differences to pointwise prediction differences. Consequently the entire learned function reaches the optimal function at the parameter stopping time. Absolute value also gives a global loss certificate. Finally, a signed pair of shifted rectifiers represents the clipped tropical map exactly, identifying a common primitive in tropical dynamics and ReLU computation.

\section{Model and empirical loss}

In max-plus algebra, tropical addition is maximum and tropical multiplication is ordinary addition.

\begin{definition}[Tropical translation model]
For $\theta\in\R$, define
\[
 f_\theta(z)=z+\theta,\qquad z\in\R.
\]
This is a max-plus monomial, hence a tropical polynomial and a tropical rational function.
\end{definition}

The model can be read as residual correction. For observations $(z_i,y_i)$, set $r_i=y_i-z_i$. Then $|f_\theta(z_i)-y_i|=|\theta-r_i|$, so fitting the offset under absolute loss estimates a robust center of the residuals.

Fix ordered reduced targets $a\le m\le c$.

\begin{definition}[Three-point absolute loss]
The empirical loss is
\[
 \loss(\theta)=|\theta-a|+|\theta-m|+|\theta-c|.
\]
\end{definition}

This objective is convex and piecewise affine, with possible corners at the three targets.

\begin{lemma}[Median optimality and uniqueness]\label{lem:median}
If $a\le m\le c$, then $\loss(m)\le\loss(\theta)$ for every $\theta\in\R$. If $\theta$ globally minimizes $\loss$, then $\theta=m$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Suppose $\theta\ge m$. The first and middle absolute deviations increase by $\theta-m$, while the third can decrease by at most $\theta-m$. Thus
\[
 \loss(\theta)-\loss(m)\ge \theta-m,
\]
which is nonnegative and is positive if $\theta>m$. The case $\theta\le m$ is symmetric and yields $\loss(\theta)-\loss(m)\ge m-\theta$. Hence equality at a global minimum forces $\theta=m$.
\end{proof}

\section{Clipped tropical descent}

\begin{definition}[Clipped tropical flow]
For a target $m$, travel budget $t\in\R$, and starting point $x\in\R$, define
\[
 G_m(t,x)=
 \begin{cases}
  \min\{m,x+t\},&x<m,\\
  \max\{m,x-t\},&x\ge m.
 \end{cases}
\]
For optimization, $t\ge0$.
\end{definition}

The flow moves toward $m$ at unit speed and is absorbed there. The min and max shorten the last movement when the remaining distance is below the travel budget.

\begin{theorem}[Exact distance law]\label{thm:distance}
For all $m,t,x\in\R$,
\[
 |G_m(t,x)-m|=\max\{0,|x-m|-t\}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If $x<m$ and $x+t\ge m$, clipping gives $G_m(t,x)=m$ and both sides vanish. If $x+t<m$, then
\[
 |G_m(t,x)-m|=m-x-t=|x-m|-t>0.
\]
For $x\ge m$, either $x-t\le m$ and both sides vanish, or $x-t>m$ and
\[
 |G_m(t,x)-m|=x-m-t=|x-m|-t>0.
\]
The maximum combines the captured and uncaptured cases.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Discrete clipped descent]
Given step size $\eta\in\R$, initial parameter $x$, and $n\in\mathbb{N}$, define
\[
 \theta_n=G_m(n\eta,x).
\]
For $\eta\ge0$, this is equivalent to repeatedly moving by at most $\eta$ toward $m$.
\end{definition}

\begin{corollary}[Exact discrete convergence rate]\label{cor:rate}
For every $n\in\mathbb N$,
\[
 |\theta_n-m|=\max\{0,|x-m|-n\eta\}.
\]
In particular, if $|x-m|\le n\eta$, then $\theta_n=m$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Substitute $t=n\eta$ in Theorem~\ref{thm:distance}. If the right-hand side is zero, zero absolute distance forces equality with $m$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Finite termination]\label{thm:termination}
Let $\eta>0$. Then
\[
 \theta_n=m\quad\text{for all}\quad
 n\ge N:=\left\lceil\frac{|x-m|}{\eta}\right\rceil.
\]
Consequently, $\theta_n\to m$ as $n\to\infty$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The ceiling property gives $|x-m|\le N\eta$. For $n\ge N$, positivity of $\eta$ gives $N\eta\le n\eta$. Corollary~\ref{cor:rate} then yields $\theta_n=m$. Eventual equality implies convergence.
\end{proof}

Positivity is essential: with $\eta=0$, the parameter stays at $x$, and a negative step has no descent interpretation.

\section{Convergence of the learned function}

\begin{lemma}[Parameter--prediction isometry]\label{lem:isometry}
For all $\theta,m,z\in\R$,
\[
 |f_\theta(z)-f_m(z)|=|\theta-m|.
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The input cancels because $(z+\theta)-(z+m)=\theta-m$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Convergence of trained tropical rational functions]\label{thm:function}
If $\eta>0$, then for every $z\in\R$,
\[
 \lim_{n\to\infty}f_{\theta_n}(z)=f_m(z)=z+m.
\]
More precisely,
\[
 |f_{\theta_n}(z)-f_m(z)|
 =\max\{0,|x-m|-n\eta\}.
\]
The equality is independent of $z$, so convergence is also uniform on every subset of $\R$ and terminates at time $N$ from Theorem~\ref{thm:termination}.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Combine Lemma~\ref{lem:isometry} with Corollary~\ref{cor:rate}. Once the parameter is $m$, the two functions agree on every input.
\end{proof}

\section{Explicit empirical-loss rate}

\begin{lemma}[Three-sample loss stability]\label{lem:lipschitz}
For every $a,m,c,\theta\in\R$,
\[
 \loss(\theta)-\loss(m)\le3|\theta-m|.
\]
No ordering assumption is needed for this upper bound.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
For any fixed $r$, the reverse triangle inequality gives
\[
 |\theta-r|-|m-r|\le|\theta-m|.
\]
Apply it for $r=a,m,c$ and add the three inequalities.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Explicit training-loss rate]\label{thm:loss}
Suppose $a\le m\le c$. For every $n\in\mathbb N$,
\[
 0\le \loss(\theta_n)-\loss(m)
 \le3\max\{0,|x-m|-n\eta\}.
\]
For $\eta>0$, the excess loss is zero for every $n\ge\lceil|x-m|/\eta\rceil$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Lemma~\ref{lem:median} gives the lower bound. Lemma~\ref{lem:lipschitz} bounds the excess loss by $3|\theta_n-m|$, and Corollary~\ref{cor:rate} supplies the exact distance.
\end{proof}

The Lipschitz bound is a certificate and need not be an equality. In contrast, the parameter and prediction formulas are exact.

\section{Exact representation by two ReLU units}

Let $\relu(u)=\max\{0,u\}$.

\begin{theorem}[Two-ReLU representation]\label{thm:relu}
For $t\ge0$ and all $m,x\in\R$,
\[
 G_m(t,x)=m+\relu(x-m-t)-\relu(m-x-t).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If $x>m+t$, only the first rectifier is active, and the expression is $x-t$. If $m-t\le x\le m+t$, both are zero, and the expression is $m$. If $x<m-t$, only the second is active, and the expression is $x+t$. These are exactly the three branches of the clipped flow.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[ReLU realization of every iterate]
If $\eta\ge0$, then
\[
 \theta_n=m+\relu(x-m-n\eta)-\relu(m-x-n\eta)
\]
for every $n\in\mathbb N$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Set $t=n\eta$ in Theorem~\ref{thm:relu}; this value is nonnegative.
\end{proof}

The signed pair of hinges creates two affine tails and a central plateau. Thus the tropical min/max description and a width-two rectified-linear description define the same map, not merely approximations of one another.

\section{Main learning theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Convergence and optimality of tropical translation training]\label{thm:main}
Let $a,m,c,x,\eta\in\R$ satisfy $a\le m\le c$ and $\eta>0$. Define $f_\theta(z)=z+\theta$, $\loss(\theta)=|\theta-a|+|\theta-m|+|\theta-c|$, and $\theta_n=G_m(n\eta,x)$. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
 \item $f_{\theta_n}(z)\to f_m(z)$ for every $z\in\R$;
 \item $f_{\theta_n}=f_m$ for every $n\ge\lceil|x-m|/\eta\rceil$;
 \item $m$ is the unique global minimizer of $\loss$;
 \item for every $z$,
 \[
 |\theta_n-m|=|f_{\theta_n}(z)-f_m(z)|
 =\max\{0,|x-m|-n\eta\};
 \]
 \item the excess loss satisfies the bounds in Theorem~\ref{thm:loss};
 \item every iterate has the exact two-ReLU representation above.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The first two claims follow from finite parameter termination and the parameter--prediction isometry. Median optimality gives the third. The exact distance theorem and loss stability give the fourth and fifth. The final claim is Theorem~\ref{thm:relu} at cumulative time $n\eta$.
\end{proof}

\section{Algorithms and complexity}

The iterative algorithm initializes $\theta\leftarrow x$. At each update, it replaces $\theta$ by $\min\{m,\theta+\eta\}$ when $\theta<m$, and by $\max\{m,\theta-\eta\}$ otherwise. Each update takes constant time and constant auxiliary space. Producing $K$ iterates costs $O(K)$ time and $O(1)$ space, or $O(K)$ storage when the trajectory is retained.

When only the $n$th iterate is required, direct evaluation of $G_m(n\eta,x)$ takes $O(1)$ arithmetic operations and $O(1)$ space. The stopping time $N=\lceil|x-m|/\eta\rceil$ is likewise available before iteration. The min/max update snaps exactly to $m$ in exact arithmetic and avoids overshoot even for a very large positive step.

\section{Numerical examples}

Take $a=-2$, $m=1$, $c=5$, $x=-4$, and $\eta=2$. Since $|x-m|=5$, the stopping time is $\lceil5/2\rceil=3$. The trajectory is
\[
 -4,\ -2,\ 0,\ 1,\ 1,\ldots,
\]
and the exact distances are $5,3,1,0,0,\ldots$. At $z=7$, predictions are $3,5,7,8,8,\ldots$. The losses are $20,14,10,8$, while the corresponding excess-loss certificates are $15,9,3,0$ above the minimum value $8$.

For motion from above, let $m=0$, $x=5.5$, and $\eta=1.5$. The iterates are $5.5,4,2.5,1,0$. The fourth movement is clipped from length $1.5$ to length $1$. If instead $x=-4$, $m=1$, and $\eta=100$, the optimizer lands at $m$ in one update; no upper restriction on the positive step is needed.

\section{Applications, limitations, and discussion}

The model describes robust offset calibration. Given residuals $r_i=y_i-z_i$, absolute-loss fitting chooses their median and is less sensitive to extreme observations than mean-based fitting. The tropical formulation is relevant when max-plus structure or a large-weight limit creates piecewise-affine regions. Here the dynamical states are below the expanding capture interval, inside it, and above it.

The ReLU identity offers a complementary interpretation: a pair of shifted hinges builds the optimizer map itself. This does not imply that arbitrary ReLU training has identical dynamics; it identifies an exact common primitive in this scalar setting.

The conclusions rely on a scalar translation model and three unweighted absolute deviations. Coupled parameters need not evolve coordinatewise, other losses may select other location statistics, and finite-weight models may perturb the ideal piecewise-linear dynamics. The loss bound is global and convenient but not always sharp.

Several extensions follow the same geometry. For $2k+1$ ordered residuals, absolute loss again selects the unique central order statistic. For $2k$ residuals, minimizers form the interval between the central two order statistics, suggesting finite capture by an interval. In a separable $d$-parameter model, the maximum coordinatewise arrival time should govern termination.

If each update is perturbed by at most $\varepsilon<\eta$, the guaranteed progress outside a neighborhood should be $\eta-\varepsilon$, suggesting
\[
 |\theta_n-m|\le\max\{0,|x-m|-n(\eta-\varepsilon)\}
\]
until entry into a closed $\varepsilon$-neighborhood. Finally, when $t>0$, the clipped flow has two breakpoints, whereas one ordinary ReLU has at most one. Together with Theorem~\ref{thm:relu}, this motivates a sharp width-two minimality theorem.

\section{Conclusion}

Clipped fixed-step descent for a tropical translation model admits a complete solution. Three-sample absolute loss selects the median uniquely. The optimizer approaches that median with an exact linear distance law, reaches it after finitely many updates, and remains there. Parameter error equals prediction error, excess loss has an explicit vanishing certificate, and the trajectory is represented by two shifted ReLU units. Piecewise-linear structure thus turns a qualitative convergence question into an exact identity and links robust statistics, optimization dynamics, and neural representation.

